41 < 1 ## The tsconfig gotcha
42 < 1 
43 < 1 Take the Bun types from the `@types/bun` devDependency and name them in
44 < 1 tsconfig:
45 < 1 
46 < 1 ```json
47 < 1 { "compilerOptions": { "types": ["bun"] } }
48 < 1 ```
49 < 1 
50 < 1 The older `bun-types` package name fails with
51 < 1 `TS2688: Cannot find type definition file for 'bun-types'`.
52 < 1 
53 < 1 ## The store, and the TinyApp shape
54 < 1 
55 < 1 The app's state is **one TinyBase store** (operator, 2026-09-08). Every `do:` in a
56 < 1 Claim is a store mutation through the app's own callbacks and every `see:` is a
57 < 1 store read plus a render against it, so an exam can probe the store directly,
58 < 1 in-process, in milliseconds — no network or DOM event loop in the loop. That
59 < 1 holds exactly when the app reads from nowhere but the store; a plan that has
60 < 1 the app fetch or read outside its persister has left the shape and says so.
61 < 1 
62 < 1 A **TinyApp** is the synced shape of that stack, and only that shape: a TinyBase
63 < 1 `MergeableStore` in the client, a `WsSynchronizer` to a Durable Object with a
64 < 1 SQLite persister, scaffolded by the official generator as a typed project —
65 < 1 
66 < 1 ```sh
67 < 1 npm create tinybase@latest -- --non-interactive --projectName <name> \
68 < 1   --language typescript --framework react --schemas true \
69 < 1   --syncType durable-objects --persistenceType sqlite --installAndRun false
70 < 1 ```
71 < 1 
72 < 1 — so the store's schema drives TypeScript inference and `bunx tsc --noEmit`
73 < 1 checks every data access (operator, 2026-09-09, chosen over a single-file
74 < 1 `index.html` + `app.jsx` that no compiler can see). Read
75 < 1 https://tinybase.org/skills/build-with-tinybase/SKILL.md before scaffolding; the
76 < 1 generator's `--list-options` is the authority for current values. An app whose
77 < 1 store is local-only is a Bun + TypeScript + TinyBase target, not a TinyApp. Write the
78 < 1 word `TinyApp` for that shape and nothing else; the borrowed term "vibes app"
79 < 1 is not this project's vocabulary.
80 < 1 
81 < 1 A TinyApp's server is **one root Durable Object per app instance** — `AppRoot`,
82 < 1 reached as `env.APP.getByName('<instance>')` — and it is the only object the
83 < 1 outside world addresses. Each store is a **module object** of its own: a
84 < 1 `WsServerDurableObject` whose `createPersister()` is
85 < 1 `createDurableObjectSqlStoragePersister(store, this.ctx.storage.sql, { mode: 'fragmented' })`,
86 < 1 named `<instance>/<module>[@label]` — the optional `@label` names a fork of that
87 < 1 module — and reached only through the root, which maps `/sync/<module>` to
88 < 1 `env.MODULES.getByName('<instance>/<module>')` and serves the exam surface beside
89 < 1 it. The parcelling follows the plan's: one task owns one module, so two tasks
90 < 1 never write one store, and the client's `WsSynchronizer` dials `/sync/<module>`
91 < 1 for the module it reads.
92 < 1 
93 < 1 A Durable Object **Facet** is not that shape while celld cannot carry a facet's
94 < 1 WebSocket. celld runs facets (`ctx.facets`, on 0.5.0 and later; each with its own
95 < 1 SQLite file since 0.6.0), and a facet answers a plain request. But on celld 0.6.1 the
96 < 1 `101` a facet returns still reaches the client without its upgrade (`Missing
97 < 1 upgrade header`), both for a facet started from a loaded Worker and for one
98 < 1 started from the app's own class through `ctx.exports`, while a named Durable
99 < 1 Object's socket echoes and closes cleanly on the same build (macOS arm64,
100 < 1 2026-10-01, n=1 run each; denoland/celld#210 was closed as not planned on
101 < 1 2026-09-17). A plan that wants facets keeps them behind a flag, and only for
102 < 1 verbs that are not sockets.
103 < 1 
104 < 1 A **stories-v1** plan does not scaffold with the generator: its launch base is
105 < 1 `skills/ultrawrite/stories/tinyapp-template/` written by `stories/scaffold.ts`
106 < 1 (a React 19 page, one `AppStore` Durable Object with the SQLite persister). It
107 < 1 is still a TinyApp; builders write each piece's screen as a json-render spec and
108 < 1 no page code, as `## Screens` says (operator, 2026-10-01).
109 < 1 
110 < 1 **Who is signed in** comes from Cloudflare Access, never from the app: the
111 < 1 template's Worker answers `/me` with the email `ctx.access.getIdentity()` vouches
112 < 1 for (null when Access did not run), the page keeps it as the hook's `who`, and
113 < 1 every store tool gets it as `run`'s third argument, so a role rule lives in the
114 < 1 signed store module. A story step's `"as": "<email>" | null` sets who is signed
115 < 1 in from that step on; the checker sets the hook's `who` the same way. The rule
116 < 1 runs in the page, so it stops the screens and the page's tools, not a client
117 < 1 that writes the store directly; a server-side lock is its own piece (radio rs4,
118 < 1 2026-09-28).
119 < 1 
120 < 1 ## Going online
121 < 1 
122 < 1 A TinyApp goes online as **one Cloudflare Worker** that serves both the page and
123 < 1 the store (operator, 2026-09-29). The template's `scripts/pack-client.ts`
124 < 1 (`bun run pack`) builds `client/index.html` and writes every output file into
125 < 1 `server/client-files.ts`, and `bun run deploy` runs `pack-client` before
126 < 1 `wrangler deploy`, so there is no second host for the page. The Worker answers
127 < 1 these routes and no others:
128 < 1 
129 < 1 - `/` — the page, public and read-only: it mounts only the pieces whose spec
130 < 1   says `"public": true` and re-reads `/public.json` every 5 s instead of syncing.
131 < 1 - `/public.json` — the saved snapshot of the store, read-only.
132 < 1 - `/health` — `200` when the Worker is up; what a plan's Verify line reads.
133 < 1 - `/staff` — the same page with every piece, its WebMCP tools and the sync.
134 < 1 - `/sync` — the store's `WsSynchronizer` socket.
135 < 1 - `/me` — who is signed in, as `## The store, and the TinyApp shape` says.
136 < 1 
137 < 1 **Cloudflare Access** guards `/staff`, `/sync` and `/me`, and leaves `/`,
138 < 1 `/public.json` and `/health` public. The operator sets that Access application
139 < 1 in the Cloudflare dashboard; no plan does, and no task writes Access config.
140 < 1 
141 < 1 A plan marks a piece a visitor may see with `"public": true` in its spec,
142 < 1 `client/src/pieces/<piece>.json`; a piece without it is mounted only under
143 < 1 `/staff`. A public piece renders on both, and hides its staff controls with
144 < 1 `visible` on `/session/staff` — so the public page is the same screens with the
145 < 1 write controls gone, not a second app.
146 < 1 
147 < 1 A plan that publishes carries two header lines, verbatim:
148 < 1 
149 < 1 - **Publish:** bun install && bun run deploy
150 < 1 - **Verify:** curl -fsS "$ULTRA_PUBLISH_URL/health"
151 < 1 
152 < 1 and sets the Worker's `name` in `server/wrangler.jsonc`. The Cloudflare account
153 < 1 goes in the app's `deploy` script, never in the config: `"deploy": "bun
154 < 1 scripts/pack-client.ts && CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=<id> bunx wrangler deploy --config
155 < 1 server/wrangler.jsonc"`. celld refuses `account_id` and `workers_dev` in the config
156 < 1 it starts from, and the story checker runs every step under celld (radio-station,
157 < 1 2026-09-29: `celld deploy does not support these config keys: account_id,
158 < 1 workers_dev`). The Publish line runs once the plan's tasks have merged; the Verify
159 < 1 line exits 0 only when the deployed Worker answers `/health`.
160 < 1 
161 < 1 ## Screens
162 < 1 
163 < 1 A TinyApp's screens are built from **1st-Pouf**, not hand-written page code
164 < 1 (operator, 2026-10-01; this replaces the 2026-09-16 Tailwind + shadcn styling
165 < 1 experiment).
166 < 1 
168 > 1 - **The spec.** The page is one screen, `client/src/screen.json`, a
169 < 1   json-render spec — `{root, elements, public?}`, where `public` lists the
170 < 1   element ids whose subtrees a signed-out visitor sees on `/` — over the catalog in
167 < 2 - **The spec.** Each piece's screen is `client/src/pieces/<piece>.json`, a
168 < 2   json-render spec — `{root, elements}`, plus `"public": true` for a piece a
169 < 2   signed-out visitor sees on `/` — over the catalog in
170 < 1   `client/src/screens/catalog.ts`: 1st-Pouf's pieces with Zod props, and the
172 > 1   store-bound `ActionButton`, `ActionCheckbox`, `DraftInput` and `DraftNumber`. The page is
171 < 2   store-bound `ActionButton`, `ActionCheckbox` and `DraftInput`. The page is
173 > 1   React 19; a builder writes specs, and a part only when a story needs one.
174 < 1 - **The parts list grows in the open (operator, 2026-11-20, #1491).** Each part has a
175 < 1   line in `client/src/screens/draws.json` naming the accessibility role it draws and
176 < 1   the props that name it (`ActionButton`: a button, by `name` then `label`). A story
177 < 1   that expects to see a role no part draws is a `PARTS fact:` while planning, and
178 < 1   compile tells that piece's task to add the part; any builder whose screen needs one
179 < 1   adds it: its props in `catalog.ts`, a wrapper over a 1st-Pouf component in
180 < 1   `registry.tsx`, its `draws.json` line, and any label function in `functions.ts`. The
181 < 1   PR lists the run's new parts under `### New screen parts`; one worth keeping is copied
182 < 1   into the template by hand, so the next app starts with it. Before this, the parts were
183 < 1   closed to a run, and potluck-2 run-1 (2026-11-20) ended a draft on a status label no
184 < 1   part could draw.
172 < 2   React 19; a builder writes specs, not components.
174 > 1 - **Where the spec starts.** The screen starts as the whole page the operator
175 < 1   picked while planning (`<bundle>/screens/screen.json`, which compile copies
176 < 1   in), else the scaffold's empty column; every piece's builder edits it from there.
173 < 2 - **Where a spec starts.** A piece's spec starts as the screen the operator
174 < 2   approved while planning (`<bundle>/screens/<piece>.json`, which compile copies
175 < 2   in), else the scaffold's empty column; the builder edits it from there.
177 > 1 - **The reads.** A row shows its own cells; a row naming another table's row (a tag's
178 < 1   `todoId`) reads that row's text through the `$computed` functions `lookup` and `format` in
179 < 1   `client/src/screens/functions.ts`, which every renderer is given (#1524).
176 < 1 - **The writes.** Saved data changes only through card actions, run from an
177 < 1   element's `action` prop. `$bindState` and json-render's built-in state
179 > 1   actions write only under `/draft/` (a built-in with no `statePath` writes its own
180 < 1   default, so it names one), and no screen binds `$bindItem`, which writes the row.
178 < 2   actions write only under `/draft/`.
179 < 1 - **The look.** 1st-Pouf (moji2002/1st-pouf, MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Mojtaba
180 < 1   Beheshti) is vendored byte-identical at `client/src/pouf/` from commit
181 < 1   `1993b1ec9f2d93b6753929d1f70c89f8ef89b13e`, pinned by
183 > 1   `client/src/pouf.vendor.json`, and never patched; its `blocks/` and `demos/`
184 < 1   folders, which nothing imports, are left out (the manifest's `omitted`). Tailwind v4 compiles its
182 < 2   `client/src/pouf.vendor.json`, and never patched. Tailwind v4 compiles its
183 < 1   theme at build through `bun-plugin-tailwind` in `scripts/build-client.ts`,
184 < 1   which `bun run pack` and the story checker both run. The font is
185 < 1   `@fontsource-variable/nunito`, bundled, so no font host is dialled.
186 < 1 - **The check.** `bun factory/stack/tinyapp/screens.ts (--plan <plan.md> |
187 < 1   --bundle <dir>) [--copy <app>] [--piece <piece>]` checks every spec without a
188 < 1   browser (exit 0 pass, 1 finding, 2 could not run); the engine runs it as one
189 < 1   more fact of each piece's task, so a missing or miswired button is caught
191 > 1   there. It also checks the parts: every part has its `draws.json` line, and
192 < 1   `client/src/pouf/` is byte-for-byte its pin.
190 < 2   there.
